WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.260
Settle in, this is unintended consequences episode 37 for June 22, 2026. Monday, a fresh

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:15.880
case to chew on as the week begins. In 2007, Indonesian officials approved new palm oil

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:21.640
concessions across central Kalimantan that would eventually cover more than 2 million hectares.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:27.840
Those approvals came with long planning horizons because the land had to be cleared, drained

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:34.660
where necessary and planted before any oil could flow. European refineries had already signed

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:41.160
long-term supply contracts for the crude palm oil those plantations would produce because EU

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:47.860
rules counted the resulting biodiesel as a low carbon fuel. The contracts locked in volumes years

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:53.000
ahead of actual harvest, creating a guaranteed buyer for whatever came off the new ground.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.360
Within five years, satellite records showed that roughly one-third of the new planting

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:04.600
sat on land that had been primary forest only months earlier. The speed of that shift surprised

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:11.260
observers who had assumed existing degraded land would absorb most of the expansion. The arithmetic

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:18.920
here matters if one-third of two million hectares moved from forest to plantation. That single wave

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:26.820
alone represented on the order of 650,000 hectares of direct forest loss tied to the policy-driven

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:33.840
demand. In the mid-2000s, both the European Commission and the United States Congress faced rising

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.740
oil prices and mounting evidence that transport sector carbon dioxide was still climbing.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:45.200
Officials looked for a drop-in substitute that could use existing engines and pipelines

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.760
without requiring new vehicle fleets or refueling infrastructure.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:56.400
Biofuels made from crops appear to offer a closed carbon cycle. Plads absorbed carbon dioxide

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:04.760
while growing, then released it when burned. The 2005 US Energy Policy Act and the 2009 EU

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:12.300
Renewable Energy Directive therefore set binding volume targets. These included 36 billion gallons

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:20.700
of renewable fuel in the United States by 2022 and 10% renewable energy in transport across the EU by

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:28.340
2020. At the time, lifecycle models used by both governments treated land use change as a minor

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:35.520
or zero factor, so the arithmetic showed large net savings. A model that assigns zero emissions

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:41.720
to forest clearing will, by definition, report bigger reductions than one that adds those emissions

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:48.740
back in. The US Renewable Fuel Standard required refiners to blend increasing shares of corn,

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:56.320
ethanol, and advanced biofuels, backed by tax credits of 51 cents per gallon. That credit reduced

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:02.220
the effective cost of each gallon blended, which in turn encouraged refiners to meet the rising

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:09.240
percentage mandates rather than pay penalties. In Europe, the fuel quality directive and national

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:15.600
blending mandates created similar demand for fatty acid methyl esters derived from vegetable oils.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:24.000
By 2010, US corn acreage had risen by roughly 10 million acres compared with 2005 levels.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:33.140
Indonesia's palm oil output doubled between 2005 and 2012. Trade data from that period show that more

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:38.760
than half of Europe's palm oil imports were ultimately routed to biodiesel plants in Rotterdam

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:44.520
and Antwerp. Proponents cited falling tailpipe emissions in regulatory filings.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:50.240
Early government reports recorded compliance without yet measuring distant land conversion,

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:57.000
because the models simply did not include a line item for peat drainage or forest clearing at the time.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:03.080
Lifecycle studies published after 2008, beginning with Searchinger's Science Paper,

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:09.580
and followed by multiple EU commissioned reviews, recalculated emissions once peat soil drainage

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:15.840
and forest clearing were included. On tropical peat lands, the release of stored carbon turned

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:24.700
a nominal 30-50% saving into a net increase that could exceed 100% relative to gasoline over a 30-year

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:31.720
horizon. The key step in that recalculation is the oxidation of carbon that had been locked in

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:38.100
peat for centuries. Once the water table drops, that carbon begins to leave the soil as carbon

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:44.880
dioxide at rates that dwarf the annual uptake of the palm trees planted on top. In Indonesia and

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:51.760
Malaysia, the area of industrial palm oil concessions expanded by an estimated 6 million hectares

00:04:51.760 --> 00:05:00.100
between 2000 and 2010. Roughly 20% of that expansion occurred on previously forested land according

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:08.380
to satellite analyses. The resulting smoke from peat fires in 2015 alone produced more carbon dioxide

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:15.320
in a single season than Germany's annual industrial emissions. Corn ethanol expansion in the Midwest,

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:23.060
meanwhile, displaced soybean acreage, which in turn encouraged additional soil planting in Brazil's

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:30.100
Cerrado and Amazon margins. These indirect effects were not captured in the original regulatory models,

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:35.960
so the very policies meant to reduce atmospheric carbon added measurable increments instead.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:43.660
By 2015, the EU had introduced an indirect land-use change factor into its accounting rules

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:49.680
and caped the contribution of food-based biofuels at 7% of transport energy.

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:55.800
The United States Environmental Protection Agency adjusted its greenhouse gas thresholds

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:01.880
for new biofuel pathways, effectively slowing approvals for additional corn ethanol capacity.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:07.660
Palm oil, biodiesel, lost its advanced status in some member states.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:14.240
Several large European buyers shifted contracts toward certified sustainable sources or waste

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:21.780
oils. Even so, existing plantations continued to operate and enforcement of zero deforestation commitments

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:30.220
remained uneven. Current EU targets now emphasize waste-based and advanced fuels, yet legacy demand from

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:37.160
earlier mandates still shapes global vegetable oil markets. Incentive structures that reward a single

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:43.680
measured attribute, tailpipe carbon intensity, while leaving other variables unpriced will reliably

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:50.280
shift pressure onto those variables. Complex ecological and economic systems respond through channels

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:56.720
that are visible only after the policy is already locked in. When designing future climate or energy

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:03.260
rules, therefore it is worth asking which unmeasured margins the new incentive will enlarge and whether

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:09.520
those margins can be monitored before the next planting season begins. That's the case for today.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:14.960
The lessons tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone who'd find it

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:20.700
useful and subscribe wherever you're listening. See you tomorrow, and before you go, this show is

